Right-pair insertion sends a right-pair token to insertion of its inner token and sends the bottom, an atom, and a left-pair token to the identity. That operation is left commutative, by casing on the two tokens and using commutativity of insertion when both are right-pair tokens, with the remaining cases definitionally equal. The right pair projection of a finite set of tree tokens is the fold of this insertion over the underlying multiset, starting from the empty set.

A tree token lies in such a fold if and only if the multiset contains the corresponding right-pair token. Consequently, a tree token lies in the right pair projection of a finite set if and only if the corresponding right-pair token lies in the set.

The fold claim is proved by induction on the multiset. The empty multiset has empty fold and contains no token. If the head is a right-pair token, membership in the inserted fold is equality with the inner token or membership in the fold of the tail, and the inductive hypothesis matches a witness in the tail with a witness in the cons, while a witness at the head is that right-pair token. If the head is the bottom, an atom, or a left-pair token, insertion acts as the identity, so membership is membership in the tail fold, and the head itself cannot be the desired right-pair token. The projection claim rewrites along this characterization: a witness equal to the right-pair token is an element of the finite set, and the right-pair token itself is such a witness.
